% Device teardown discipline, run against a live device (-d <dev>) by
% run-device-destroy-test.sh: prints SUCCESS iff every step survives.
%
% The device contract requires Destroy to be idempotent: the interpreter
% retires an outgoing device at setpagedevice and again at job end, and a
% program may have called Destroy itself. A device holding raw resources
% (a framebuffer, a window connection, a libpng writer) must clear the
% stored handle so a repeated Destroy is a no-op, not a double free.
%
% Draw and emit a page first so devices that finalise output in Destroy
% (png) exercise their real teardown path, then Destroy twice explicitly;
% normal job-end teardown makes a third call on the way out.

newpath 10 10 moveto 90 90 lineto stroke showpage

/.destroydevice {
    .privatedict /.graphicsdict get /currgstate get /device get
    dup /Destroy 2 copy known
    { get exec }
    { pop pop pop }
    ifelse
} def

.destroydevice
.destroydevice

count 0 eq { (SUCCESS) = } { (FAILURES: operand stack unbalanced) = } ifelse
